// 63b — is a USER FUNCTION CALL legal in a guard?
//
// Ticket 63's motivating example is `when not IsAdmin(u)`. That example assumes
// the un-negated form `when IsAdmin(u)` is legal. Every guard in the shipped
// example corpus is a comparison; not one calls a function. This file asks the
// compiler directly.
//
// Erlang forbids it — a guard admits only guard BIFs, never a user function —
// and B# emits Erlang Abstract Format, so the restriction is inherited unless
// the compiler refuses it earlier with a better message.
module GuardProbe

public atom IsAdmin(int u)
IsAdmin(1) -> :yes
IsAdmin(_) -> :no

// The un-negated form. If THIS is refused, ticket 63's example was never
// available with or without a `not`, and item 1 is asking about a construct that
// does not exist rather than about negation.
public atom Check(int u)
Check(u) when IsAdmin(u) == :yes -> :admin
Check(_)                         -> :ordinary
